Here is the core technical functionality formatted in the style of an official patent Abstract and Independent Claims, using standard patent numbering and legalese.

***

**TITLE OF THE INVENTION**
SYSTEM AND METHOD FOR CROSS-PERSPECTIVE SPATIAL INSTRUCTION MAPPING IN INDUSTRIAL WORKSPACES

### **ABSTRACT**
A system (**100**) for cross-perspective visual instruction sharing within an industrial workspace (**102**) includes a plurality of optical sensors (**104a**, **104b**) having disparate fields of view of a target object (**106**), a spatial mapping processor (**108**), and a display interface (**110**). The spatial mapping processor (**108**) is configured to receive a user input indicating a visual instruction marker (**112**) at a first 2D coordinate within a first image feed captured by a first optical sensor (**104a**). The processor (**108**) maps the first 2D coordinate to a global 3D spatial coordinate using a 3D workspace model (**114**), and subsequently transforms the global 3D spatial coordinate into a second 2D coordinate calibrated to the viewpoint of a second optical sensor (**104b**) or user display interface (**110**). The system (**100**) renders a perspective-corrected instance of the visual instruction marker (**112**) overlaid on the target object (**106**) in real-time, compensating for parallax and occlusion across differing camera perspectives.

---

### **PATENT CLAIMS**

**What is claimed is:**

**1. A cross-perspective spatial instruction system (100) comprising:**
*   a first optical sensor (**104a**) positioned to capture a first image feed of an industrial workspace (**102**) from a first viewpoint;
*   at least a second optical sensor (**104b**) positioned to capture a second image feed of the industrial workspace (**102**) from a second viewpoint distinct from the first viewpoint;
*   a memory (**116**) storing a dynamic three-dimensional (3D) spatial representation (**114**) of the industrial workspace (**102**); and
*   a spatial mapping processor (**108**) communicatively coupled to the first optical sensor (**104a**), the second optical sensor (**104b**), and the memory (**116**), the processor (**108**) configured to:
    *   receive a user interaction designating a visual instruction marker (**112**) at a first pixel coordinate set within the first image feed;
    *   project said first pixel coordinate set into the 3D spatial representation (**114**) to derive a global 3D anchor coordinate associated with a target component (**106**);
    *   calculate a transformation matrix based on extrinsic calibration parameters between the first optical sensor (**104a**) and the second optical sensor (**104b**);
    *   map said global 3D anchor coordinate to a second pixel coordinate set corresponding to the field of view of the second optical sensor (**104b**) using the transformation matrix; and
    *   generate a graphic overlay comprising the visual instruction marker (**112**) scaled, oriented, and rendered at the second pixel coordinate set for display on a user interface (**110**).

**2. A computer-implemented method for sharing visual instruction markers across heterogeneous camera perspectives, the method comprising:**
*   ingesting, via a network interface, a first image frame from a primary capture device (**104a**) and a second image frame from a secondary capture device (**104b**);
*   identifying a target point within the industrial workspace (**102**) selected by a user on the first image frame;
*   querying a spatial depth engine (**118**) to assign a 3D point cloud vector to the target point based on volumetric data stored in a workspace database (**114**);
*   evaluating occlusion boundaries of the target component (**106**) relative to the secondary capture device (**104b**) using ray-tracing analysis against the 3D point cloud vector;
*   transforming the 3D point cloud vector into a secondary 2D frame-of-reference corresponding to the view matrix of the secondary capture device (**104b**); and
*   transmitting a graphical payload to a visual display unit (**110**) to render a synthetically projected instruction marker (**112**) anchored to the target component (**106**) in the secondary 2D frame-of-reference, wherein the instruction marker (**112**) is continuously stabilized against motion artifacts of the secondary capture device (**104b**).
